# Coach speech (Dutch): the building blocks every line uses (src/coach/catalog.cpp renders them).
# Same keys as assets/coach/speech/en/common.lang, plus the grammatical forms Dutch needs.
# Register: the coach says "je" to the player ("jij" / "jouw" only when stressed, for a contrast), and
# "ik" / "mijn" for himself; the game menus say "je" too. Dutch adjectives, participles and past tenses
# never agree with a person, so the player's gender never shows. Vocabulary (KNSB usage, as in
# assets/i18n/nl.lang): zet, veld, lijn, rij, schaak, mat, pat, remise, rokade, slaan, penning, vork, spies.

# ---- Pieces --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Nouns: de pion, het paard, de loper, de toren, de dame, de koning. Genders (piece.<type>.gender):
# m, n, m, m, f, m. Dutch has no cases: one form is both subject and object ("{your} valt {my} aan",
# "Sla {my}!"). What depends on the piece is the article (de / het), the pronouns that point back at it
# (the knight is "het", the queen "ze" / "haar", the others "hij" / "hem") and an adjective after "een".
# Forms (every one defined for all six pieces and the four owners your, my, white, black; {your2},
# {my2}, {t1}, {t2} and the analysis {piece} take the same forms). Never write je / mijn / de / het
# before a piece placeholder, except before the form "bare", which has no determiner of its own:
#   nom    je paard / mijn toren / het witte paard / de zwarte dame (the default, "{your}")
#            "{your} staat gepend: als {your:subj} weggaat, staat je koning schaak."
#   emph   jouw paard / mijn toren / white and black as nom (stressed possessive, for a contrast)
#            "Niet mijn loper maar {your:emph} staat aangevallen."
#   def    het paard / de toren (definite article, owner dropped)
#            "Breng {your} naar voren! In het eindspel is {your:def} een sterk stuk."
#   art    het / de (the article alone, to put an adjective before the noun)
#            "{piece:art} ongedekte {piece:bare} gaat verloren." (analysis: the side is dropped)
#   bare   paard / toren (the noun alone, after a determiner or adjective you write yourself)
#            "Je gepende {your:bare} kan {sq} niet dekken.", "Pak mijn ongedekte {my:bare}!"
#   subj   het / hij / ze (subject pronoun; capitalised at the start of a sentence)
#            "Dit is {your}. {your:subj} beweegt schuin, zo ver als je wilt."
#   obj    het / hem / haar (object pronoun)
#            "{my} staat ongedekt. Sla {my:obj}!", "{your2} dekte {your}; nu slaat {my} {your:obj}."
#   poss   zijn / haar ("its")
#            "Je koning staat naast {my}. Ga op {my:poss} veld staan om {my:obj} te slaan."
#   rel    dat / die (relative pronoun; also the demonstrative "that one", which spoken Dutch often
#          prefers to hij / het as a subject)
#            "{reply} slaat {piece}, {piece:rel} geen verdediger had."
#            "Kijk naar {your}: {your:rel} kan nergens heen."
#            With bare, the demonstrative determiner: "{your:rel} {your:bare}" = "dat paard" / "die loper".
#   this   dit / deze (demonstrative "this", alone or with bare)
#            "{your:this} {your:bare} moet nu weg."
#   e      (nothing) / e: the ending of an adjective after een, geen, elk, welk, zo'n (a het-word has none;
#          after de, het, je, mijn, dit, dat the adjective always ends in -e)
#            "Een gepend{your:e} {your:bare} is een slechte verdediger." ("Een gepend paard",
#            "Een gepende loper")
#   alone  het jouwe / de jouwe, het mijne / de mijne, het witte / de witte ("yours", "mine"), only when the
#          noun was just said for a piece of the SAME type
#            "Jouw dame staat aangevallen, {my:alone} niet." (my queen: "de mijne")
# After a preposition Dutch uses "er" + preposition for a thing, whatever its gender: "erachter",
# "ernaast", "ermee", "erop" ("{t2} staat erachter"), and "waar" + preposition in a relative clause
# ("waarop", "waarmee"); never "achter hem" / "naast het". Nothing else in Dutch agrees with a piece:
# adjectives after a verb, participles and verbs stay the same ("{your} is gepend", "{my} is gevallen").
piece.pawn.gender = m
piece.pawn.nom = pion
piece.pawn.emph = pion
piece.pawn.def = pion
piece.pawn.art = de
piece.pawn.bare = pion
piece.pawn.subj = hij
piece.pawn.obj = hem
piece.pawn.poss = zijn
piece.pawn.rel = die
piece.pawn.this = deze
piece.pawn.e = e
piece.pawn.alone = pion
piece.knight.gender = n
piece.knight.nom = paard
